// Texts of any length: what keeps the bytes of a `text` without a
// capacity. A module that has such a text imports this one:
//
//   import text
//   component Page { words: text }
//
// Most of it is not called: the compiler does it. Where a field is
// given a text it asks for a block with a copy of the bytes, and where a
// field gets another, its component goes or its entity is destroyed, it
// gives the block back; `len`, `t[i]`, `==` and `as text[N]` look into
// one. All of that is in devices/text.c, which is built with a program
// that imports this module, like any module's C.
//
// A program run with ENT_TEXT_REPORT set says at its end how many blocks
// its fields held.
//
// What a program calls: fns over texts. Where one gives a text it is a
// new one (as joining makes, kept while the schedule runs; a field that
// is given it takes its copy), or the one it was given where that is the
// same. Byte numbers start at 0.

// Where `what` is in `t` first, from byte `from` on (from 0 where `from`
// is below nought); -1 where it is not there. An empty `what` is at
// `from`, where that is no further than the end of `t`.
extern fn find(t: text, what: text, from: i32) -> i32
// The bytes of `t` from byte `from` up to byte `to`, not that one: those
// of them it has (none where `to` is not after `from`).
extern fn slice(t: text, from: i32, to: i32) -> text
// `t` with A to Z as a to z; every other byte as it is.
extern fn lower(t: text) -> text
// The whole number `t` starts with (after spaces, with a sign), as
// strtoll reads it; 0 where it starts with none.
extern fn number(t: text) -> i64
